1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,314
Welcome to lesson one of the Kubernetes course.

2
00:00:03,414 --> 00:00:10,768
In this lesson, you'll learn what containers are, why Kubernetes depends on them, and how to run and build your first container.

3
00:00:12,118 --> 00:00:14,064
Why start with containers?

4
00:00:14,164 --> 00:00:18,030
Because Kubernetes runs containers. That's its whole job.

5
00:00:18,130 --> 00:00:25,804
If you don't understand what a container is, every later lesson will feel like magic. So let's build a solid foundation first.

6
00:00:27,154 --> 00:00:29,564
Here's the problem containers solve.

7
00:00:29,664 --> 00:00:32,522
A developer says: it works on my machine.

8
00:00:32,622 --> 00:00:39,768
But the server has a different operating system, different library versions, or different settings, so the same code breaks there.

9
00:00:41,118 --> 00:00:42,952
The solution is a container.

10
00:00:43,052 --> 00:00:50,406
A container packages your app together with everything it needs: the runtime, the libraries, and the minimal operating system files.

11
00:00:50,506 --> 00:00:57,060
That same package runs the same way on a laptop, a test server, or in production, isolated from other apps.

12
00:00:58,410 --> 00:01:00,484
There are five terms you must know.

13
00:01:00,584 --> 00:01:05,602
An image is a read-only template. Think of it as a recipe, or a class in programming.

14
00:01:05,702 --> 00:01:11,792
A container is a running instance of an image, like a cooked meal, or an object created from a class.

15
00:01:11,892 --> 00:01:15,358
A Dockerfile is the set of instructions used to build an image.

16
00:01:15,458 --> 00:01:19,116
A registry is where images are stored, such as Docker Hub.

17
00:01:19,216 --> 00:01:23,914
And a tag is a version label, like nginx:1.27.

18
00:01:25,264 --> 00:01:28,042
How is a container different from a virtual machine?

19
00:01:28,142 --> 00:01:35,752
Each virtual machine includes a whole operating system of its own. That makes it gigabytes in size, and it takes minutes to start.

20
00:01:35,852 --> 00:01:40,646
Containers share the host's kernel, so there's no separate operating system to boot.

21
00:01:40,746 --> 00:01:46,180
They're megabytes in size and start in seconds. That's why Kubernetes can run thousands of them.

22
00:01:47,530 --> 00:01:50,996
Now let's practice. You don't need to install anything.

23
00:01:51,096 --> 00:01:57,586
Open the Killercoda Ubuntu playground in your browser. It gives you a free Linux terminal with Docker already installed.

24
00:01:57,686 --> 00:01:59,376
Follow along with each step.

25
00:02:00,726 --> 00:02:03,632
Step one: run your first container.

26
00:02:03,732 --> 00:02:12,366
Type: docker run -d --name web -p 8080:80 nginx:1.27

27
00:02:12,466 --> 00:02:14,732
-d runs it in the background.

28
00:02:14,832 --> 00:02:17,178
--name gives it the name web.

29
00:02:17,278 --> 00:02:22,280
-p maps port 8080 on your machine to port 80 inside the container.

30
00:02:22,380 --> 00:02:26,758
And nginx:1.27 is the image, with its version tag.

31
00:02:28,108 --> 00:02:30,406
Step two: check that it's running.

32
00:02:30,506 --> 00:02:40,132
docker ps lists running containers. Then curl localhost:8080. You should see the page that says "Welcome to nginx!"

33
00:02:40,232 --> 00:02:42,306
Step three: look inside.

34
00:02:42,406 --> 00:02:45,264
docker logs web shows the container's logs.

35
00:02:45,364 --> 00:02:54,702
docker exec -it web sh opens a shell inside the running container. List the web files, then type exit to leave.

36
00:02:56,052 --> 00:02:58,334
Step four: build your own image.

37
00:02:58,434 --> 00:03:03,548
Create a folder called myapp, and a small index.html file with a heading.

38
00:03:03,648 --> 00:03:13,402
Then write a Dockerfile with two lines. FROM nginx:1.27, which is the base image. And COPY, which puts your index file into the folder nginx serves.

39
00:03:13,502 --> 00:03:18,664
Build it with docker build -t myapp:1.0 .

40
00:03:18,764 --> 00:03:24,710
Run it on port 8081, and curl it. You'll see your own page, served from your own image.

41
00:03:26,060 --> 00:03:27,862
Step five: clean up.

42
00:03:27,962 --> 00:03:33,588
docker rm -f web mine stops and removes both containers.

43
00:03:33,688 --> 00:03:40,418
Containers are disposable. You can remove them and start fresh at any time, because the images stay on your machine.

44
00:03:41,768 --> 00:03:45,282
Here are the Dockerfile instructions you'll see most often.

45
00:03:45,382 --> 00:03:51,824
FROM picks the base image. WORKDIR sets the working directory. COPY copies files from your machine into the image.

46
00:03:51,924 --> 00:03:57,438
RUN executes a command while the image is being built, for example installing packages.

47
00:03:57,538 --> 00:03:59,996
ENV sets an environment variable.

48
00:04:00,096 --> 00:04:06,410
EXPOSE only documents which port the app uses. It doesn't publish the port. That's what -p does.

49
00:04:06,510 --> 00:04:10,648
USER makes the app run as a normal user instead of root, which is safer.

50
00:04:10,748 --> 00:04:14,310
And CMD is the command that runs when a container starts.

51
00:04:15,660 --> 00:04:19,446
One distinction to remember: build time versus run time.

52
00:04:19,546 --> 00:04:25,396
At build time, FROM, COPY and RUN create the image. That happens once per version.

53
00:04:25,496 --> 00:04:30,914
At run time, CMD starts the container. That happens every time a container starts.

54
00:04:32,264 --> 00:04:33,490
Let's recap.

55
00:04:33,590 --> 00:04:38,336
A container packages an app with everything it needs, so it runs the same everywhere.

56
00:04:38,436 --> 00:04:42,078
An image is the template, and a container is a running instance of it.

57
00:04:42,178 --> 00:04:48,012
Containers share the host's kernel, which makes them small and fast compared with virtual machines.

58
00:04:48,112 --> 00:04:52,826
You've used docker run, ps, logs, exec, build and rm.

59
00:04:52,926 --> 00:04:57,368
And remember to pin image versions with tags, and avoid running as root.

60
00:04:57,468 --> 00:05:03,734
In the next lesson, we'll cover the Linux and networking basics you need for Kubernetes. See you there.
